      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-6100",
      ":question/problem": "Differentiate $y = \\cos^4{2x}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny = u^4, \\qquad u = \\cos{v}, \\qquad v = 2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 4u^3,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d v} = -\\sin{v},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm d x} = 2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x)^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =-8{cos}^{3}\u20612x\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
